    "lawCode": "Amd §§6-132, 6-140, 6-204, 6-206 & 15-108, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S218\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to requirements of\nwitnesses to a designating petition or independent nominating petition\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo delete the requirement that a witness to a party designating\npetition or an independent nominating petition must reside in the\ndistrict of the office in the petition.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends subdivisions 2 and 3 of section 6-132 of\nthe election law, as amended by chapter 447 of the laws of 2006, to\nclarify the requirement that the witness has not previously signed a\npetition for another candidate for the same office and to delete\nlanguage that requires that a witness to a designating petition be a\nresident of the political subdivision in which the office or position\nis to be voted for in order to be qualified to witness the petition.\nSee Matter of DiPietro v. New York State Board of Elections, 30\nMisc.3d 449 (Erie Co. Sup. Ct. 2010).\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends subpart b of subdivision 1 and\nsubdivision 2 of section 6-140 of the election law, as amended by\nchapter 447 of the laws of 2006, to add a requirement that the witness\nhas not previously signed a petition for another candidate for the\nsame office and to delete language that requires that a witness to a\nindependent nominating petition be a resident of the political\nsubdivision in which the office or position is to be voted for in\norder to be qualified to witness the petition.\n\nSections 3, 4, 5, and 6 of the bill amend, respectively, subdivision 1\nof section 6-204, subdivision 1 of section 6-206, subdivision 3 of\nsection 15-108, and subdivision 4 of 15-108 of the election law to\ndelete language that requires that a witness to a designating petition\nbe a resident of the village in which the office or position is to be\nvoted for in order to be qualified to witness the petition.\n\nSection 7 of the bill is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe federal courts and the New York State Court of Appeals ruled\nunconstitutional the present election law's requirement that a\nsubscribing witness to a designating petition or independent\nnominating petition must be a resident of the political subdivision in\nwhich the office or position is to be voted on. This bill would\nconform the language of the election law to the court decisions.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2012: A.8650 passed assembly\n2014: A.1464 passed assembly\n2015-16: S.4420A referred to Elections, committed to Rules\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nOn the 120th day after it shall become law. Effective immediately, the\naddition, amendment and/or repeal of any rules or regulations\nnecessary for the implementation of this act on its effective date are\nauthorized to be made on or before such date.\n",
